        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "Reinforcement learning has recently seen tremendous empirical and theoretical success Mnih et al. (2015); Sutton et al. (1992); Jin et al. (2020b); Zanette et al. (2020); Gheshlaghi Azar et al. (2013); Dann & Brunskill (2015). Theoretically, near optimal algorithms have been proposed to explore and learn a given MDP with sample access to trajectories. In this work, we consider the problem of learning the optimal policies for multiple MDPs collaboratively so that the total number of trajectories sampled per MDP is smaller the number of trajectories required to learn them individually. This combines reinforcement learning and the supervised learning perspective on collaborative filtering. We assume that the various users have the same transition matrices, but different rewards and the rewards have a low rank structure in order to facilitate collaborative filtering. Low rank assumption is popular in the collaborative filtering literature and has been deployed successfully in a variety of tasks Bell & Koren (2007); Gleich & Lim (2011); Hsieh et al. (2012). This can be regarded as an instance of multi-task reinforcement, various versions of which have been considered in the literature Brunskill & Li (2013); Taylor & Stone (2009); D\u2019Eramo et al. (2020); Teh et al. (2017); Hessel et al. (2019).\nA Motivating Example Consider the example of travel itinerary/ navigation suggestion generation for multiple users, where each user has a different preference for travel. In the case of navigation, some users might prefer taking buses, some might prefer taking the metro and others might prefer to take the taxi at different spots. In the case of travel, some might like traveling to museums and big cities while some others might prefer camping.\nWe can model the state as being the current place being visited during the trip and the action as the means of transportation which changes the current state. In this scenario, each person can be assumed to have the same state-space, action space and the same state transition matrix. However, these users obtain different \u2018rewards\u2019 since they have different preferences for places and modes of transportation. We want to understand how to plan each of their travel without having each of the users visit every destination using every mode of transportation as required by the usual reinforcement learning methods.\nOur Contributions a) Improved Sample Complexity: In this work, we introduce the setting of multi-user collaborative reinforcement learning in the case of tabular MDPs and linear MDPs and\nprovide sample efficient algorithms for both these scenarios without access to a generative model. The total sample complexity required to learn the near-optimal policies for every user scales as O(N + |S||A|) instead of O(N |S||A|) in the case of tabular MDPs and O(N + d) instead of O(Nd2) in the case of linear MDPs.\nb) Collaborative Exploration: In order to reconstruct the rewards of all the users efficiently under low-rank assumptions, we need to deploy standard low rank matrix estimation/completion algorithms. These algorithms require specific kinds of linear measurements (See Section 1.1) with certain distributional properties. Without access to a generative model, the main challenge in this setting is to obtain these linear measurements by querying trajectories of carefully designed policies. In the case of Tabular MDPs, a natural and elegant algorithm (Algorithm 1) allows us to obtain uniformly distributed observations which allow efficient matrix completion.\nc) Functional Reward Maximization: In the case of linear MDPs, matrix completion is more challenging since we observe measurements of the form e\u22bai \u0398\n\u2217\u03c8 where \u0398\u2217 \u2208 RN\u00d7d, corresponding to the reward obtained by user i, with respect to an embedding \u03c8. Estimating \u0398\u2217 under low rank assumptions requires the distribution of \u03c8 to have certain isotropy properties (see Section 6). Querying such measurements goes beyond the usual reward maximization and are related to mean-field limits of multi-agent reinforcement learning similar to the setting in Cammardella et al. (2020) where a functional of the distribution of the states is optimized. Using some structural results for Linear MDPs, we design a procedure which can sample-efficiently estimate policies which lead to these isotropic measurements (Section 5).\nd) Matrix Completion With Row-Wise Linear Measurements: For the linear MDP setting, the low rank matrix estimation problem lies somewhere in between the matrix completion (Recht, 2011; Jain et al., 2013) and matrix estimation with restricted strong convexity (Negahban et al., 2009). We give a novel active learning based algorithm where we estimate \u0398\u2217 row by row without any assumptions like incoherence. This is described in Section 6."
